## Statement

Fix $a\ge100$, put $R:=\sqrt a+3\ge13$, $S:=[-R-1,R+1]^2$, and let $w$, $\delta$, $P$, $\beta_\delta$, $R_0$, the rescaled pulse equation, the endpoint map $K_\delta$ and the stroboscopic map $G_\delta=K_\delta\circ R_0$ be as in [[def-smooth-periodic-oscillator-pulse-model]]. Let

$$K(q,p):=(q,\,p+q^2-a),\qquad F(q,p):=(p,\,p^2-a-q),\qquad J:=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\-1&0\end{pmatrix},$$

so that $F=K\circ R_0$. Then there are constants $\delta_1>0$ and $C_1<\infty$ depending only on $a$ and $w$ such that for every $0<\delta\le\delta_1$:

1. **(existence on the pulse)** for every $(q,p)\in S$ the solution of the rescaled equation with initial data $(q,p)$ exists on $[0,1]$ and stays in the compact tube $T:=[-R-2,R+2]\times[-M,M]$, $M:=R+1+(R+1)^2+1$; hence $K_\delta$ is defined on $S$, $K_\delta(S)\subseteq T$, and

$$\sup_{S}\bigl(|K_\delta-K|+\|DK_\delta-DK\|\bigr)\le C_1\delta$$

for the Euclidean norm and the operator norm;
2. **(closeness of the period map)** $\sup_S\bigl(|G_\delta-F|+\|DG_\delta-DF\|\bigr)\le C_1\delta$;
3. **(smoothness, invertibility, symplecticity)** $G_\delta$ is of class $C^\infty$ on an open neighbourhood $N$ of $S$; $DG_\delta(z)$ is invertible for every $z\in N$ and $G_\delta$ is injective on $N$, so $G_\delta:N\to G_\delta(N)$ is a $C^\infty$ diffeomorphism onto its image; and

$$DG_\delta(z)^{\mathsf T}J\,DG_\delta(z)=J\qquad(z\in N),$$

that is, $G_\delta$ is symplectic;
4. **(the reverse-conjugated map)** let $S_w(q,p):=(p,q)$ and $H_\delta:=S_w\circ G_\delta^{-1}\circ S_w$ on a neighbourhood of $S$. Then $H_\delta$ is of class $C^\infty$ there, and there is $C_2<\infty$, depending only on $a$ and $w$, with

$$\sup_{S}\bigl(|H_\delta-F|+\|DH_\delta-DF\|\bigr)\le C_2\delta .$$

Consequently, for every $\delta$ small enough that $\varepsilon:=C_2\delta$ satisfies $\varepsilon\le1/6$ and $C\varepsilon\le1$ ($C$ the constant of the perturbed-recurrence lemma on this page), the maps $G_\delta$ and $H_\delta$ satisfy all hypotheses of the perturbed horseshoe theorem on this page on the square $S$.

## Facts & Assumptions

**Given:** The parameter $a\ge100$, the constants $R,M$, the normalized pulse $w$ of [[def-smooth-periodic-oscillator-pulse-model]], a number $\delta>0$, and the maps $K,F,R_0,K_\delta,G_\delta,S_w,H_\delta$; write $\bar W:=\max(1,\sup|w|,\sup|w'|)$.

[F1] Rescaled field data ([[def-smooth-periodic-oscillator-pulse-model]], [[thm-cm-compact-continuation-for-locally-lipschitz-odes]], [[def-locally-lipschitz-in-the-state-variable]]): $f_\delta(s,q,p)=(\delta p,\,-\delta q+w(s)(q^2-a))$ and $f_0(s,q,p)=(0,\,w(s)(q^2-a))$ are continuous and locally Lipschitz in the state; on $T$ one has $|f_\delta-f_0|\le\delta M$, $\|Df_\delta-Df_0\|\le\bar W\delta$, $\|Df_\delta\|\le1+2\bar W(R+2)=:L_1$ for $\delta\le1$, and the second state derivatives are bounded by $2\bar W$ on $T$; solutions have unique maximal intervals.

[F2] The reference solutions ([[def-darboux-integral]], [[thm-continuous-implies-integrable]], [[thm-linearity-of-the-integral]], [[thm-monotonicity-of-the-integral]], [[def-abs-value]], [[def-interval]]): $z_0(s)=(q_0,\,p_0+(q_0^2-a)\int_0^sw)$ solves $z'=f_0(s,z)$, $z(0)=(q_0,p_0)$, and for $(q_0,p_0)\in S$ satisfies $|q_0|\le R+1$, $|p_0|\le R+1$, $|q_0^2-a|\le(R+1)^2$ and hence $z_0([0,1])\subseteq[-R-1,R+1]\times[-M+1,M-1]\subseteq T$.

[F3] Gronwall comparison ([[thm-gronwall-integral-inequality]], [[def-metric-space]]): if $u\ge0$ is continuous with $u(s)\le A+B\int_0^su$ on $[0,1]$, then $u(s)\le Ae^{Bs}$.

[F4] The variational equation ([[thm-c1-dependence-of-solutions-on-initial-data]]): the solution map is $C^1$ in the initial data on a common interval, and its derivative matrix solves $Y'=D_zf_\delta(s,z_\delta(s))Y$, $Y(0)=I$; the analogous statement holds for $f_0$.

[F5] Smooth dependence ([[thm-smooth-dependence-of-ode-solutions-on-parameters]]): the solution depends smoothly on initial data and on parameters on a common compact interval; here the parameter is $\delta$, entering the rescaled field polynomially, and $s$ ranges over the compact interval $[0,1]$.

[F6] Calculus on the square ([[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]], [[def-total-derivative-in-euclidean-space]], [[def-jacobian-matrix-and-gradient]], [[thm-chain-rule-for-total-derivatives]], [[thm-mean-value-inequality-for-total-derivatives]], [[def-continuity-real]], [[lem-of-abs-value]], [[def-metric-ball]]): the chain rule for the composition $K_\delta\circ R_0$ and $S_w\circ K_\delta^{-1}\circ S_w$, the mean value inequality for Lipschitz bounds on the convex square, and the inverse-differentiation identity $(A^{-1})'(z)=-(A^{-1}A'A^{-1})(A^{-1}(z))$ for an invertible differentiable map.

[F7] Inversion estimates ([[lem-newton-contraction-near-an-invertible-derivative]], [[thm-real-square-matrix-invertible-iff-determinant-nonzero]], [[cor-inverse-matrix-by-adjugate]], [[thm-higher-regularity-of-local-inverses]], [[thm-extreme-value-metric]]): if $f$ is $C^1$ near $a$ with $A:=Df(a)$ invertible and $\|A^{-1}\|\le C_A$, and if $\|Df(z)-A\|\le\frac1{2C_A}$ for $|z-a|\le R_1$, then for every $y$ with $|y-f(a)|\le R_1/(2C_A)$ the Newton map $T_y(z):=z+A^{-1}(y-f(z))$ maps the complete convex ball $\bar B(a,R_1)$ into itself and is a contraction with constant $1/2$, so $f$ has a unique preimage $z$ of $y$ there with $|z-a|\le2C_A|y-f(a)|$; if in addition $f$ is $C^\infty$, that local inverse is $C^\infty$ with derivative $(Df)^{-1}$ at the image point. A real $2\times2$ matrix is invertible exactly when its determinant is nonzero, and the inverse is then $\det^{-1}$ times the adjugate; the adjugate of $DK_\delta(q,p)$ has entries bounded by $2R+3$ on the square, so $\|(DK_\delta)^{-1}\|\le4(2R+3)$ whenever $|\det DK_\delta-1|\le1/2$.



## Proof

**Proof technique:** direct; compare the $\delta$-rescaled integral equations with the explicit $\delta=0$ solutions by Gronwall, subtract the variational equations for the derivative comparison, and read off smoothness, invertibility and symplecticity from the rescaled flow.

1.1 Reference curve bounds: for $(q_0,p_0)\in S$, [F2] gives $|q_0|\le R+1$, $|p_0|\le R+1$ and $|q_0^2-a|\le(R+1)^2$ (because $a\le(R+1)^2$ as $R+1\ge14$), and $w\ge0$, $\int_0^1w=1$; hence $|p_0+(q_0^2-a)\int_0^sw|\le(R+1)+(R+1)^2=M-1$ for all $s\in[0,1]$. So $z_0([0,1])$ lies in $[-R-1,R+1]\times[-M+1,M-1]$, at distance at least $1$ from the boundary of $T$. [given, F2, algebra]

2.1 Gronwall estimate for the state: let $z_\delta$ be the solution of $z'=f_\delta(s,z)$, $z(0)=(q_0,p_0)$, as long as it stays in $T$. Using [F1] and [F2], $$|z_\delta(s)-z_0(s)|\le\int_0^s\Bigl(\bigl|f_\delta(\tau,z_\delta)-f_\delta(\tau,z_0)\bigr|+\bigl|f_\delta(\tau,z_0)-f_0(\tau,z_0)\bigr|\Bigr)d\tau\le\int_0^s\bigl(L_1|z_\delta-z_0|+\delta M\bigr)d\tau,$$ so [F3] gives $|z_\delta(s)-z_0(s)|\le\delta M\,e^{L_1}=:C_0\delta$ for all $s$ in the interval of residence in $T$. [given, step 1.1, F1, F2, F3, algebra]

2.2 Uniform bounds on a larger square: put $S':=[-R-\tfrac14,R+\tfrac14]^2$. The estimates proved above are uniform on $S'$ with a possibly larger constant, still called $C_1$: for initial data in $S'$ the reference solution of [F2] satisfies $|q_0|,|p_0|\le R+\tfrac14$ and $|q_0^2-a|\le(R+\tfrac14)^2$ (because $a\le(R+\tfrac14)^2$), so it stays at distance at least $\tfrac34$ from the boundary of $T$; the Gronwall comparison, the first-exit argument and the variational comparison therefore apply verbatim, and by [F5] all second derivatives of $z\mapsto K_\delta(z)$ on the compact square $S'$ are bounded by a constant $M_2<\infty$ depending only on $R$ and $w$, uniformly for $0<\delta\le1$ ([[thm-extreme-value-metric]]). In particular $$\sup_{S'}\bigl(|K_\delta-K|+\|DK_\delta-DK\|\bigr)\le C_1\delta,\qquad \sup_{S'}\bigl(|G_\delta-F|+\|DG_\delta-DF\|\bigr)\le C_1\delta .$$ [given, step 1.1, F1, F2, F3, F5, F6, F7, algebra]

3.1 First-exit argument: choose $\delta_1\le1$ so small that $C_0\delta_1\le1/2$. If, for some $\delta\le\delta_1$, the solution left $T$ at a first time $s_*$, then step 2.1 would give $|z_\delta(s_*)-z_0(s_*)|\le1/2$, while $z_0(s_*)$ is at distance at least $1$ from the boundary of $T$ by step 1.1 and $z_\delta(s_*)$ is on it: a contradiction. Hence $z_\delta$ exists on $[0,1]$ and stays in $T$, and $|z_\delta(1)-z_0(1)|=|K_\delta(q_0,p_0)-K(q_0,p_0)|\le C_0\delta$. [step 1.1, step 2.1, F1, algebra]

3.2 Invertibility: on $S'$ write $DK(q,p)=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\2q&1\end{pmatrix}$ and $E:=DK_\delta-DK=(e_{ij})$, so that $|e_{ij}|\le C_1\delta$. Since $\det DK\equiv1$, expanding the determinant of $DK_\delta=DK+E$ gives $$\det DK_\delta-1=e_{11}+e_{22}+e_{11}e_{22}-2q\,e_{12}-e_{12}e_{21},$$ so $|\det DK_\delta-1|\le(2(R+\tfrac14)+4)C_1\delta+2(C_1\delta)^2\le(2R+6)C_1\delta\le\tfrac12$ once $C_1\delta\le\min(\tfrac12,\tfrac1{2(2R+6)})$. By [F7] this makes $DK_\delta(q,p)$ invertible, with $\|(DK_\delta(q,p))^{-1}\|\le4(2R+3)$ on $S'$. As $DR_0=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\-1&0\end{pmatrix}$ is an isometry, $DG_\delta=DK_\delta\circ DR_0$ is invertible at every point $z$ with $R_0z\in S'$; in particular on the open square $N:=(-R-\tfrac18,R+\tfrac18)^2$, which satisfies $R_0(N)=N\subseteq S'$. [given, step 2.2, F6, F7, algebra]

4.1 Derivative comparison: let $Y_\delta$ and $Y_0$ solve $Y'=Df_\delta(s,z_\delta(s))Y$, $Y(0)=I$ and $Y'=Df_0(s,z_0(s))Y$, $Y(0)=I$; both exist on $[0,1]$ by [F4] (the solutions exist on that interval by step 3.1), and $\|Y_\delta(s)\|\le e^{L_1s}$ by [F3] and [F1]. Since $Df_0$ is Lipschitz in the state with constant $2\bar W$ on the convex tube $T$ ([F1], mean value inequality), [F1] and step 2.1 give $$\bigl\|Df_\delta(s,z_\delta(s))-Df_0(s,z_0(s))\bigr\|\le\bar W\delta+2\bar W C_0\delta=:C_0'\delta .$$ Subtracting the two integral equations and applying [F3] yields $\|Y_\delta(s)-Y_0(s)\|\le C_0'\delta\,e^{L_1s}\cdot e^{L_1s}\le C_1\delta$ on $[0,1]$ after absorbing constants. Hence, by [F4], $DK_\delta-DK=(Y_\delta(1)-Y_0(1))$ is bounded by $C_1\delta$ in operator norm on $S$, which together with step 3.1 proves clause 1. [step 2.1, step 3.1, F1, F3, F4, algebra]

4.2 Smoothness: the rescaled field $(s,q,p,\delta)\mapsto f_\delta(s,q,p)$ is $C^\infty$ on the open tube, so by [F5] the solution map $(s,q,p,\delta)\mapsto(Q_\delta(s),P_\delta(s))$ is $C^\infty$ on a neighbourhood of $[0,1]\times[-R-1,R+1]^2$; restricting to $s=1$ makes $K_\delta$, and hence $G_\delta$, $C^\infty$ on a neighbourhood $N$ of $S$. [given, step 3.1, F5, F6]

4.3 Injectivity: let $u,v\in S'$. The polynomial map $K$ has the exact expansion $K(x,y)=(x,y+x^2-a)$, so $$K(u)-K(v)=M(u,v)(u-v),\qquad M(u,v)=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\u_1+v_1&1\end{pmatrix},$$ and $M(u,v)^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\-(u_1+v_1)&1\end{pmatrix}$ has norm at most $1+2(R+\tfrac14)\le2R+3$; by the mean value inequality on the convex square $S'$, $|(K_\delta-K)(u)-(K_\delta-K)(v)|\le C_1\delta\,|u-v|$. If $K_\delta(u)=K_\delta(v)$, then $u-v=-M(u,v)^{-1}\bigl[(K_\delta-K)(u)-(K_\delta-K)(v)\bigr]$, so $|u-v|\le(2R+3)C_1\delta\,|u-v|\le\tfrac12|u-v|$ provided $(2R+3)C_1\delta\le\tfrac12$, forcing $u=v$; hence $K_\delta$ is injective on $S'$, and since $R_0$ maps $S$ into itself, $G_\delta=K_\delta\circ R_0$ is injective on $N$. By step 3.2 the differential of $G_\delta$ is invertible on $N$; by [F7] ([[thm-higher-regularity-of-local-inverses]] applied with every $k$, $K_\delta$ being $C^\infty$) the local inverses are $C^\infty$, and injectivity on $N$ makes them a single $C^\infty$ inverse on $G_\delta(N)$. Hence $G_\delta|_N$ is a $C^\infty$ diffeomorphism onto its image. [given, step 2.2, F6, F7, algebra]

5.1 Composition: $DR_0=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\-1&0\end{pmatrix}$ is an isometry of $\mathbb R^2$ in the Euclidean norm, and $G_\delta=K_\delta\circ R_0$, $F=K\circ R_0$, so the chain rule gives $|G_\delta-F|\le C_1\delta$ and $\|DG_\delta-DF\|\le C_1\delta$ on $S$. This is clause 2. [step 4.1, F6, algebra]

5.2 Symplecticity: on the original time-dependent system, let $\Phi_{t,0}$ be the flow of $\dot z=J\nabla H_\delta(t,z)$ and $Y(t):=D_z\Phi_{t,0}(z_0)$. By [F4], $Y'=A(t)Y$ with $A(t)=J\,\mathrm{Hess}\,H_\delta(t,\Phi_{t,0}(z_0))$; since $\mathrm{Hess}\,H_\delta$ is symmetric and $J^{\mathsf T}=-J$, $J^2=-I$, one computes $A^{\mathsf T}J+JA=0$, so $(Y^{\mathsf T}JY)'=Y^{\mathsf T}(A^{\mathsf T}J+JA)Y=0$ and $Y(0)=I$ give $Y^{\mathsf T}JY=J$. Thus every time slice of the Hamiltonian flow is symplectic; the free rotation (the flow of $H=(q^2+p^2)/2$ over time $\pi/2$) and the rescaled pulse flow (the flow of $h(s,q,p)=\delta(p^2+q^2)/2+w(s)(aq-q^3/3)$, whose equations are exactly $q_s=\delta p$, $p_s=-\delta q+w(s)(q^2-a)$) are symplectic, and so is their composition $G_\delta$. [given, step 4.2, F4, F6, algebra]

5.3 Closeness of the inverse: let $X:=K^{-1}(S)$; this is a compact subset of the open square $S'$ whose distance to the complement of $S'$ is positive, so there is $R_1>0$ with $C_AM_2R_1\le\tfrac12$, where $C_A:=4(2R+3)$, such that the closed $R_1$-neighbourhood of $X$ lies in $S'$. For $x\in X$ put $A:=DK_\delta(x)$, so that $\|A^{-1}\|\le C_A$ by step 3.2, and for $z\in\bar B(x,R_1)$ the mean value inequality and step 2.2 give $\|A-DK_\delta(z)\|\le M_2R_1\le\tfrac1{2C_A}$. Then the Newton map $T_y(z):=z+A^{-1}(y-K_\delta(z))$ satisfies $\|DT_y(z)\|=\|I-A^{-1}DK_\delta(z)\|\le\tfrac12$ on the complete convex ball $\bar B(x,R_1)$, so $T_y$ is a contraction there with constant $\tfrac12$ ([[thm-mean-value-inequality-for-total-derivatives]], [F7]). Let $y\in S$ and $x:=K^{-1}(y)\in X$. Then $|y-K_\delta(x)|=|K(x)-K_\delta(x)|\le C_1\delta\le R_1/(2C_A)$ for $C_1\delta\le R_1/(2C_A)$, so $|T_y(x)-x|\le C_A C_1\delta\le R_1/2$, and $T_y$ maps $\bar B(x,R_1)$ into itself; by [F7] it has a unique fixed point $x_\delta\in\bar B(x,R_1)$, i.e. $K_\delta(x_\delta)=y$, with $|x_\delta-x|\le2C_A C_1\delta$. Therefore $K_\delta^{-1}$ is defined on the open set $U:=\bigcup_{x\in X}B(K_\delta(x),R_1/(2C_A))$, which contains $S$, and $\|K_\delta^{-1}(y)-K^{-1}(y)\|_2\le2C_A C_1\delta$ for $y\in S$. Differentiating the identity $K_\delta(K_\delta^{-1}(y))=y$ gives $D(K_\delta^{-1})(y)=(DK_\delta)^{-1}(x_\delta)$ by [F7], while $D(K^{-1})(y)=(DK)^{-1}(x)=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\-2x_1&1\end{pmatrix}$. With the identity $B^{-1}-A^{-1}=B^{-1}(A-B)A^{-1}$, $\|(DK)^{-1}\|\le2R+3$ and $\|(DK_\delta)^{-1}\|\le C_A$ on $S'$, one bounds $\|(DK_\delta)^{-1}(x_\delta)-(DK)^{-1}(x_\delta)\|\le(2R+3)C_AC_1\delta$ and $\|(DK)^{-1}(x_\delta)-(DK)^{-1}(x)\|\le2|x_\delta-x|\le4C_AC_1\delta$. Hence there is $C_2'<\infty$ with $$\sup_S\bigl(|K_\delta^{-1}-K^{-1}|+\|DK_\delta^{-1}-DK^{-1}\|\bigr)\le C_2'\delta .$$ Finally, $S_w(q,p)=(p,q)$ and $R_0$ are linear isometries satisfying $S_w\circ R_0^{-1}=R_0\circ S_w$, $S_w(S)=S$ and $R_0(S)=S$; hence on $S$ $$H_\delta=S_w\circ G_\delta^{-1}\circ S_w=R_0\circ S_w\circ K_\delta^{-1}\circ S_w,\qquad F=S_w\circ F^{-1}\circ S_w=R_0\circ S_w\circ K^{-1}\circ S_w,$$ so composing with the fixed isometries preserves all norms and $\sup_S(|H_\delta-F|+\|DH_\delta-DF\|)\le C_2\delta$ with $C_2:=2C_2'$. Moreover $H_\delta$ is defined and $C^\infty$ on the open neighbourhood $S_w(U)$ of $S$ ([F7]). [given, step 4.3, F2, F6, F7, algebra]

6.1 Applicability: let $\delta_5:=\min\bigl(\delta_1,\tfrac12,\tfrac1{2C_1},\tfrac1{2C_1(2R+6)},\tfrac1{2C_1(2R+3)},\tfrac{R_1}{2C_AC_1}\bigr)$ and $C':=\max(C_1,C_2)$. For $0<\delta<\delta_5$ the estimates above hold, $G_\delta|_N$ is a $C^\infty$ diffeomorphism onto its image and $H_\delta$ is defined and $C^\infty$ on a neighbourhood of $S$ and diffeomorphic onto its image there. On $S$, the difference $G_\delta-F$ has both coordinates bounded by $|G_\delta-F|\le C_1\delta\le C'\delta$, and each of the four entries of $DG_\delta-DF$ is bounded by the operator norm $\|DG_\delta-DF\|\le C'\delta$; the same holds for $H_\delta-F$ with constant $C_2\le C'$. Hence each of the six componentwise closeness inequalities of the perturbed horseshoe theorem on this page holds for $G_\delta$ on $S$ with the constant $\varepsilon:=C'\delta$, and the same six inequalities hold for $H_\delta$. Consequently, for every $\delta$ small enough that $\varepsilon=C'\delta$ satisfies $\varepsilon\le\tfrac16$ and $C\varepsilon\le1$ ($C$ the constant of the perturbed-recurrence lemma on this page), both $G_\delta$ and $H_\delta$ satisfy all hypotheses of the perturbed horseshoe theorem on this page on the square $S$; this is the final assertion. [step 5.2, step 5.3, F2, F6, algebra] ∎


## Remarks

- The constants $C_1,C_2,\delta_1$ depend on $a$ and on the fixed pulse $w$ only through $\sup|w|$, $\sup|w'|$ and the bounds proved above; no quantity depends on $\delta$ except through the explicit factor.

- The symplecticity proof is the standard variational-equation computation for a time-dependent Hamiltonian; it does not use the autonomous theory of [[prop-cm-flow-derivative-satisfies-the-canonical-matrix-identity]], because $H_\delta$ depends on time.

- The map $\delta\mapsto H_\delta$ is smooth as well; no differentiation in $\delta$ is needed below, only the $C^1$ closeness at each fixed $\delta$.
